    <desc>Find text in the output and the scrollback of a terminal.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Search for text</title>

<p>You can search the text of a terminal, including its scrollback:</p>
  <steps>
<item><p>Press the search button in the header bar, or press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>F</key></keyseq>.</p></item>
<item><p>Type what you are looking for. Every match is highlighted, and the search bar shows which match is the current one, for example <gui>3 of 17</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>Click the up arrow button to go to the previous match and the down arrow button to go to the next one, or press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>H</key></keyseq> and <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>G</key></keyseq>.</p></item>
</steps>

  <p>Click the options button in the search bar to narrow down your
  results:</p>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Match Case</gui></title>
      <p>Only find text with the same upper and lower case letters as what you
      typed.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Whole Words</gui></title>
      <p>Only find whole words. For example, a search for “cat” does not find
      “bobcat”.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Use Regular Expressions</gui></title>
      <p>Search with a regular expression pattern. The search bar tells you if
      the pattern is invalid.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <p>Press <key>Esc</key> to close the search bar, and
  <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>J</key></keyseq> to remove the highlights.</p>

  <note style="tip">
    <p>If you expect to work with a lot of output, increase the
    <link xref="pref-scrolling#lines">scrollback lines</link> so that you can
    search further back.</p>
  </note>

